/*
:name: async_mem_reset_demoted
:type: equiv
:verilog_top: async_mem_reset_demoted
:pyrope_top: async_mem_reset_demoted
*/
//
// FIXME tracker -- an ASYNC reset OF a memory is demoted to a synchronous clear.
// The reset arm clears every element (`for (i=0;i<4;i=i+1) mem[i] <= 0`), which is
// a real asynchronous reset of the array. The Pyrope below keeps it as an ordinary
// `if rstn == 0 { mem[0] = 0 ... }` in the clocked body -- i.e. SYNCHRONOUS -- so
// the array only clears on the next clock edge instead of immediately.
// Native `lhd lec` PROVES this pair under its cycle model: inou.slang demotes the
// golden's unpeelable array rung to a sync reset (async-reset-as-sync) and LEC
// lands a reset on the clock edge. Ruled 2026-09-28: LEC treats async and sync
// reset as equivalent (pass/lec/lec.md, Flop) -- no intra-cycle model, no UNKNOWN
// for a reader-demoted reset -- so the native PROVEN stays. The yosys oracle
// (lgyosys: rstn=0 between edges, the comb read q=mem[a] shows 0 on the golden)
// refutes the pair -- that is the only leg that fails, and why this stays fixme.
// Distinct from mem_write_during_reset: there the memory has no reset at all
// and loses its write GATING; here the memory's own reset loses its ASYNC-ness.
pub mod async_mem_reset_demoted::[timecheck=false](clk:Clock, rstn:U1, we:U1, a:U2, d:U4) -> (q:U4@[]) {
  reg mem:[4]U4:[ordering="old"]
  q = 0ub????
  if rstn == 0 {
    mem[0] = 0
    mem[1] = 0
    mem[2] = 0
    mem[3] = 0
  } elif we != 0 {
    mem[a] = d
  }
  q = mem[a]
}
